yesterday there was some very  interesting hearings uh in washington dc  uh sort of primarily about the janu what  happened on january 6th and a little bit  later we're going to get to the comments  from donald trump's former acting  uh secretary of defense about when  donald trump finally did say  okay let's bring in the national guard  to clean up what's going on on january  6. we'll get to that later  but one of the really interesting i  don't know if we would call it a side  story it's really kind of a main story  that came out yesterday during the  testimony is that the biden  administration's top law enforcement  officials  including merrick garland um uh the  attorney general  including homeland security secretary  mallorcas and a number of others  they said in no uncertain terms  that white supremacists are the greatest  domestic terrorist threat within the  united states now  if you've been watching my show for a  while or if you're familiar with the  data  on domestic terrorism this would not be  a surprise to you  the story that the right has been  telling for a very long time  is that american muslims  or immigrants from muslim countries even  left-wing eco-terrorists in some  versions of the story are  really the great domestic threat but  when you look at the data  and you look at domestic extremism  incidents you very quickly understand  that the biggest threat  in the united states indeed is white  supremacists  which sometimes overlap with these sort  of patriot and sovereign citizen  anti-government type movements that's  really where the bulk of the threat  comes from and merrick garland said  yesterday to lawmakers quote  domestic violent extremists pose an  elevated threat in 2021  and in the fbi's view the top  domestic violent extremist threat we  face  comes from racially or ethnically  motivated violent extremists  specifically those who advocate for the  superiority  of the white race um now  what you are also uh starting to see  is that republicans are having to find a  new way to even talk about this  and uh senator richard shelby for  example  instead of focusing on the level of  threat is trying to make the  administration seem biased in its  priorities and when it was richard  shelby's turn to talk to merrick garland  he asked are you investigating for  example  the protests in portland oregon with the  same  vigor that you're investigating what  happened on january 6. the implication  and wrapped up in that is the  implication and the assumption  that the portland pros protests were  left-wing  violence whereas i guess he sort of  may be conceding that the january 6th  riots were right-wing violence  and merrick garland responded very well  and said quote  we don't care what the ideology is  violations of law are pursued and are  prosecuted  i think it's fair to say that in my  career as a judge and in law enforcement  i have not seen a more dangerous threat  to democracy  than the invasion of the capital that  doesn't mean we don't focus on other  threats  as well and so we we are starting to see  this kind of shift  uh among the right where it's becoming  essentially undeniable  that the bulk of the incidents are these  right-wing patriot  sovereign citizen anti-government um  uh and certainly white supremacists  racially ethnically religiously  motivated  events but what republicans are now kind  of going back to and it's good that  they're having to change what they're  saying even though it's still bogus  is okay well maybe these are the numbers  but what about the attention that's  being paid there are political decisions  that are being made about who to  investigate who to charge etc  and that should be in that should be  looked into but it's sort of like a  welcome to our world moment this is what  many of us have been saying about the  judicial justice  system for a very very long time when  you look at  who actually uh does drugs  has drugs deals drugs versus who is  prosecuted who is policed  what are the sentences that are given  out we've been pointing out that there's  a lack of equity there for a very long  time  along racial lines for sure among class  lines as well as we've discussed so  now unable to continue denying the  empirical reality  of where the greatest number of threats  come from  they are pushed back to having to make  an argument the left has been making  about the justice system for a very long  time  i don't know where this will go but  yesterday's hearings were very  interesting  because they touched on a lot of these  different um  tangential topics they're big topics but  tangential  to the it to the day of january 6th uh  including  investigative priorities for federal law  enforcement including what the biggest  threats are  and a little bit later in the show we  will get to  some of the testimony about the nitty  gritty of what happened on january 6th  send me your thoughts on this you can  find me on twitter at dpakman and  remember that on monday memorial day  it's not this monday  it's not the next monday i think it's  the one after that  monday memorial day we're doing a  one-day membership special  single day membership bomb if you've  been thinking adam  and maybe maybe i'll sign up someday  memorial day is the day to do it because  it will be a beautiful discount okay if  you want to be notified on memorial day  just get on my newsletter at  davidpakman.com  if you ever feel like you just don't  have enough time to read all the books  you want to read  you have to check out one of my favorite  apps called blinkist blinkist takes  thousands of popular nonfiction books  and distills  each one down into an ebook or audiobook  that you can get through in just 15  minutes where you're getting all the key  takeaways from the book  just imagine how you'll be able to  expand your horizons and knowledge  by being able to soak up all of the  important insights from  10 different books in an afternoon  obviously it's per